 Rocky Mountain Penstemon is one of Colorado's finest native flowering plants — electric blue-purple flower spikes in late May–June, semi-evergreen foliage, and extreme cold and drought hardiness. Short-lived (3–5 years) but self-seeds readily to maintain the colony. Blooms on old wood. Requires excellent drainage — see Other Care for planting specs.
]

=== Watering

Use 1 × 2 gph emitter (~0.93 gal/cycle at the current 28 min/zone runtime). Rocky Mountain Penstemon is highly drought tolerant. A dry fall and winter is essential for survival in clay soil — wet crowns in cold weather are the primary cause of plant loss. Cap the emitter and stop drip support in late August.

=== Fertilization

No fertilizer. Lean soil produces the best, longest-lived plants — any fertilizer addition promotes lush growth that shortens plant lifespan dramatically. A light compost topdressing in fall is the absolute maximum.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Deadhead spent flower stalks after blooming to redirect energy and extend plant life slightly. The basal rosette remains semi-evergreen through winter — leave it. Clean up winter-damaged leaves in early spring. Let some flower stalks go to seed for colony self-renewal.

=== Wildlife Value

Rocky Mountain Penstemon is a premier hummingbird plant. The tubular blue-purple flowers from late May into June are perfectly timed to coincide with hummingbird arrival in Commerce City and are eagerly visited. Bumble bees and large native solitary bees are also important pollinators. Butterflies, particularly skippers and swallowtails, visit the flowers. The self-seeding habit of this plant means that over time it naturalizes into a colony that provides increasing wildlife value each year. Cottontail rabbits typically avoid penstemon due to its slightly resinous foliage. Seeds are occasionally taken by house finches.

=== Pests & Disease

Penstemon has very few serious pest problems when well-sited. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=penstemon+crown+rot+treatment")[Crown rot] in poorly drained clay is the overwhelmingly dominant cause of plant failure — there is no treatment, only prevention through drainage management. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=penstemon+aphids+treatment")[Aphids] may cluster on new growth — treat with insecticidal soap or a strong water spray. In general, plants that dry out between waterings and have good air circulation have minimal pest problems.

=== Other Care

Plant in a raised or sloped area with clay amended to at least 50% coarse grit and gravel, or build a dedicated rocky raised bed. Alternatively, treat as a short-lived accent and rely on self-seeding for colony continuity. Resistant to deer and rabbits.
